    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d782">

                <pb facs="1060" n="1046" sameAs="frus:frus1944v06/pg1046"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.503193/36</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2379</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-03-30">March 30,
                        1944</date>.<lb/>[Received April 20.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p rendition="#flush">Subject: Proposed Regulations Governing the Post-War
                    Investment of Foreign Capital in China.</p>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to refer to the
                    Department’s telegram 378, March 23, 1944,<anchor n="16" xml:id="d782-f1"/> in
                    regard to tentative regulations reportedly under consideration by the Chinese
                    Government to “govern the encouragement of foreign capital”, and to the
                    Embassy’s telegrams nos. 551 and 552, March 29, 1944<anchor n="17"
                        xml:id="d782-f2"/> in reply thereto, and to transmit herewith a memorandum
                    prepared by an officer of the Embassy regarding the document under reference,
                    including a translation and interpretation<anchor n="17" xml:id="d782-f3"/> of
                    the document. The substance of the memorandum was given in the Embassy’s
                    telegram no. 551, March 29, 1944.</p>
                <p>As the document was obtained in confidence and is understood to be secret, the
                    Embassy hesitated to make an official request for a copy. Upon receipt of the
                    Department’s telegram, however, an informal note was addressed to the Foreign
                    Minister requesting the desired copy. It is doubtful whether it will be
                    forthcoming; but the despatch of the note will at least have the effect of
                    drawing the matter to the attention of the Foreign Minister and let him know
                    that there is attentive foreign interest in the plans of the Party and
                    Government regarding post-war investment of foreign capital in China.</p>
                <p>The translation of the document obtained from the Embassy’s confidential source
                    is crude and some of the points undoubtedly require clarification. If the
                    Chinese text can be obtained, officially or unofficially, a more careful
                    translation will be made at the Embassy and transmitted to the Department.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>C. E. <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>



                <xi:include href="d782-a1.xml" xpointer="d782-a1"/>
            </div>
